TUnit uses `[Before(...)]` and `[After(...)]` attributes with a hook type to control scope. Here's a sketch for each:

## 1. Before/After Every Test (method-level)

```csharp
public class MyTests
{
    [Before(Test)]
    public void ResetDatabase()
    {
        // runs before each [Test] in this class
        InMemoryDb.Reset();
    }

    [After(Test)]
    public void Cleanup()
    {
        // runs after each [Test]
    }

    [Test]
    public void MyTest() { /* ... */ }
}
```

## 2. Once Per Class (class-level)

```csharp
public class MyTests
{
    [Before(Class)]
    public static void OpenFixture()
    {
        // once before the first test in this class
    }

    [After(Class)]
    public static void DisposeFixture()
    {
        // once after the last test in this class
    }

    [Test]
    public void Test1() { }
}
```

## 3. Once Per Assembly

```csharp
public class GlobalSetup
{
    [Before(Assembly)]
    public static void StartWireMock()
    {
        // once before any test in this assembly
    }

    [After(Assembly)]
    public static void StopWireMock()
    {
        // once after all tests in this assembly
    }
}
```

## 4. Once Per Test Session (entire run)

```csharp
public class SessionSetup
{
    [Before(TestSession)]
    public static void ApplyMigrations()
    {
        // very first thing in the whole test run
    }

    [After(TestSession)]
    public static void TearDownDatabase()
    {
        // very last thing
    }
}
```

## Key rules

| Scope | Attribute | Method must be |
|-------|-----------|----------------|
| Test | `[Before(Test)]` / `[After(Test)]` | Instance (non-static) |
| Class | `[Before(Class)]` / `[After(Class)]` | `static` |
| Assembly | `[Before(Assembly)]` / `[After(Assembly)]` | `static` |
| TestSession | `[Before(TestSession)]` / `[After(TestSession)]` | `static` |

The `HookType` values (`Test`, `Class`, `Assembly`, `TestSession`) come from a static import:

```csharp
using static TUnit.Core.HookType;
```

All hooks support `async Task` return types if you need asynchronous setup/teardown.
